data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"2f24c25d-a653-46d5-b5f6-a51c96bd5655\", \"excerpt\": \"\\u201cAnd concerning this, we shall see. If someone grants this too, that suffices, that they are discerning and examining and, as one might say, measuring and weighing of other things. For Chrysippus and Zeno and Cleanthes, and who is it that has written that the beginning of education is the examination of names? And concerning what does Xenophon write that he began from the examination of names, wha\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses and Enchiridion\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"093102ae-7069-57f7-9b36-3864bd877fae\", \"excerpt\": \"Cicero de fato 39. Ac mihi quidem videtur, cum duae sententiae fuissent veterum philosophorum, una eorum, qui censerent omnia ita fato fieri, ut id fatum vim necessitatis adferret \\u2014 \\u2014 altera eorum, quibus viderentur sine ullo fato esse animorum motus voluntarii, Chrysippus tamquam arbiter honorarius medium ferire voluisse, sed adplicat se ad eos potius, qui necessitate motus animorum liberatos vol\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta II \\u2014 Fragmenta Logica et Physica\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"e7b7f1b4-7256-47a3-aa75-48652180f322\", \"excerpt\": \"And when someone says to you that you know nothing, and you are not stung, for even sheep do not show the shepherds how much grass they have eaten, but having digested the pasture within, they bring forth wool and milk without. Therefore, do not display your theorems to laymen, but the works of those that have been digested. When you are simply dressed in body, do not adorn yourself on account of \", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses and Enchiridion\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"a70db269-736c-4441-9c0e-8f7ca5b9cf26\", \"excerpt\": \"II. Quo itidem modo et vim necessitatemque fati constituerit et esse tamen in nobis consilii iudiciique nostri arbitrium confirmaverit. 1 Fatum, quod heimarmenen Graeci vocant, ad hanc ferme sententiam Chrysippus, Stoicae princeps philosophiae, definit: \\\"Fatum est\\\" inquit \\\"sempiterna quaedam et indeclinabilis series rerum et catena volvens semetipsa sese et inplicans per aeternos consequentiae ord\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Noctes Atticae\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"464f33dc-6828-410d-a6f5-b62891dedc80\", \"excerpt\": \"you have been attached to these. Because of this kinship, those of us who have turned away become like wolves, faithless and treacherous and harmful, savage and bestial and untamed. We are foxes and, among living beings, misfortunes. For what else is a reviler and malicious person but a fox? Or what else is more unfortunate and lowly? Therefore, pay attention, lest you become one of these misfortu\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses and Enchiridion\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"68dbd68f-d95f-5b0a-abee-388672c9ed6d\", \"excerpt\": \"Cicero de fato 11 (ex naturalibus causis vitia nasci possunt, extirpari autem et funditus tolli \\uff0d non est id positum in naturalibus causis, sed in voluntate, studio, disciplina). Quae tolluntur omnia, si vis et natura fati ex divinationis ratione firmabitur. Etenim si est divinatio, qualibusnam a perceptis artis proficiscitur? (percepta appello, quae dicuntur Graece \\u03b8\\u03b5\\u03c9\\u03c1\\u03ae\\u03bc\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u03b1). Non enim credo null\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta II \\u2014 Fragmenta Logica et Physica\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"4c862009-c672-509f-b279-4216c11c5826\", \"excerpt\": \"Cicero de fato 20. Concludit enim Chrysippus hoc modo: Si est motus sine causa, non omnis enuntiatio, quod \\u1f00\\u03be\\u03af\\u03c9\\u03bc\\u03b1 dialectici appellant, aut vera aut falsa erit; causas enim efficientis quod non habebit, id nec verum nec falsum erit. Omnis autem enuntiatio aut vera aut falsa est. Motus ergo sine causa nullus est. 21. Quod si ita est, omnia, quae fiunt, causis fiunt antegressis. Id si ita est, fato \", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta II \\u2014 Fragmenta Logica et Physica\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"dacf9fad-4007-59aa-949a-c7659a4e5d64\", \"excerpt\": \"that something is possible\\nit is true that neither..\\nand impossible doesn't follow from possible\\nas the Cleantheans hold\\nAntipater\\nPanthoides\\nChrysippus\\nDiodorus\\n\\n1. THE MASTER ARGUMENT DEBATE\\nThis passage references the famous debate over modality. Diodorus Cronus argued that what is possible is what is or will be true. Others (Cleanthes, Chrysippus) defended alternative analyses.\\n\\n2. NAMED DISPU\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses (\\u0394\\u03b9\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u03c1\\u03b9\\u03b2\\u03b1\\u03af) - Epictetus\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"2d2eee67-6073-563d-95ce-66ffa11ce8be\", \"excerpt\": \"Gellius N. A. VII 2. Fatum, quod \\u03b5\\u1f31\\u03bc\\u03b1\\u03c1\\u03bc\\u03ad\\u03bd\\u03b7\\u03bd Graeci vocant ad hanc ferme sententiam Chrysippus, Stoicae princeps philosophiae, definit: Fatum est, inquit, sempiterna quaedam et indeclinabilis series rerum et catena, volvens semetipsa sese et implicans per aeternos consequentiae ordines, xe quibus apta nexaque est. Ipsa autem verba Chrysippi, quantum valui memoria, ascripsi, ut, sicui meum istud int\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta II \\u2014 Fragmenta Logica et Physica\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"9df8110a-4a4f-58df-88b8-9891257d44fa\", \"excerpt\": \"Greek: \\u2022 \\u1f45\\u03c4\\u03b9 \\u1f14\\u03c3\\u03c4\\u03b9 \\u03c4\\u03ad \\u03c4\\u03b9 \\u03b4\\u03c5\\u03bd\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u03cc\\u03bd - that something is possible\\n\\u2022 \\u1f14\\u03c3\\u03c4\\u03b9\\u03bd \\u1f00\\u03bb\\u03b7\\u03b8\\u1f72\\u03c2 \\u03bf\\u1f54\\u03c4\\u03b5 - it is true that neither...\\n\\u2022 \\u03ba\\u03b1\\u1f76 \\u03b4\\u03c5\\u03bd\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u1ff7 \\u1f00\\u03b4\\u03cd\\u03bd\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u03bf\\u03bd \\u03bf\\u1f50\\u03ba \\u1f00\\u03ba\\u03bf\\u03bb\\u03bf\\u03c5\\u03b8\\u03b5\\u1fd6 - and impossible doesn't follow from possible\\n\\u2022 \\u03ba\\u03b1\\u03b8\\u03ac\\u03c0\\u03b5\\u03c1 \\u03bf\\u1f31 \\u03c0\\u03b5\\u03c1\\u1f76 \\u039a\\u03bb\\u03b5\\u03ac\\u03bd\\u03b8\\u03b7\\u03bd - as the Cleantheans hold\\n\\u2022 \\u1f08\\u03bd\\u03c4\\u03af\\u03c0\\u03b1\\u03c4\\u03c1\\u03bf\\u03c2 - Antipater\\n\\u2022 \\u03a0\\u03b1\\u03bd\\u03b8\\u03bf\\u03af\\u03b4\\u03b7\\u03c2 - Panthoides\\n\\u2022 \\u03a7\\u03c1\\u03cd\\u03c3\\u03b9\\u03c0\\u03c0\\u03bf\\u03c2 - Chrysippus\\n\\u2022 \\u0394\\u03b9\\u03cc\\u03b4\\u03c9\\u03c1\\u03bf\\u03c2 - Diodorus\\n\\n1. THE MASTER ARGUMENT DEBATE\\nThis passage references the\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses\"}"}

