data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"0338fcd0-3bd0-545f-9587-57231fedd860\", \"excerpt\": \"English paraphrase of Cicero, De Fato 42-43, reporting Chrysippus' cylinder analogy. Chrysippus distinguishes the external push that sets the cylinder rolling from the cylinder's own shape, which determines how it rolls. Similarly, an external impression is the proximate cause of assent, but assent itself depends on the agent's internal character.\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Cicero, De Fato\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"464f33dc-6828-410d-a6f5-b62891dedc80\", \"excerpt\": \"you have been attached to these. Because of this kinship, those of us who have turned away become like wolves, faithless and treacherous and harmful, savage and bestial and untamed. We are foxes and, among living beings, misfortunes. For what else is a reviler and malicious person but a fox? Or what else is more unfortunate and lowly? Therefore, pay attention, lest you become one of these misfortu\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses and Enchiridion\"}"}

data: {"type": "answer_chunk", "data": "{\"type\": \"citation_found\", \"passage_id\": \"2f24c25d-a653-46d5-b5f6-a51c96bd5655\", \"excerpt\": \"\\u201cAnd concerning this, we shall see. If someone grants this too, that suffices, that they are discerning and examining and, as one might say, measuring and weighing of other things. For Chrysippus and Zeno and Cleanthes, and who is it that has written that the beginning of education is the examination of names? And concerning what does Xenophon write that he began from the examination of names, wha\", \"node_ids\": [], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"work_label\": \"Discourses and Enchiridion\"}"}

